---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="college-degrees"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para"><strong>Highest package is the single biggest salary offered to one student in a batch, and median CTC is the middle salary, where half the placed students earned more and half earned less.</strong>
    If you are comparing colleges, trust the median of your own branch over the highest package, because it shows what a typical placed student got.</p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Highest package</strong> is one student's best offer. <strong>Median CTC</strong> (cost to company) is the middle placed student's offer. The average adds every offer, so a few huge ones pull it above what most students got.</li>
      <li>Plan with the branch-wise median, and test any fee or loan against fixed pay, not the headline CTC. The median counts only placed students, so ask how many were left out.</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> ask the placement cell for three years of branch-wise median and fixed pay. Then start one piece of work you can show next to the degree. Skill and proof built early are the start of a high-income skill portfolio, and they are the part of your outcome that you control.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>If a fee or a loan hangs on these numbers, <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-for-college-students/">career counselling for college students</a> helps you make the decisions that unlock high income opportunities, such as which branch, what cost and which skills to build before you pay. The <a href="/blog/college-degrees/">College and Degrees</a> section covers the rest of the college decision.</p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="median-ctc">What is median CTC? The middle salary, in plain words</h2>

  <p>Write down the offers of all placed students and sort them from smallest to largest. The offer in the exact middle is the median CTC. LPA means lakh per annum, and one lakh is 1,00,000 rupees.</p>

  <p>Here is a small batch of nine placed students. It is an illustration, not a real college.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Who</th>
          <th>Offer in LPA</th>
          <th>What it shows</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {batchRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Who"><strong>{row.student}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Offer in LPA">{row.pay}</td>
            <td data-label="What it shows">{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The highest package is 48 LPA. The average is about 9.9 LPA, because the one big offer drags it up.</p>

  <p>The median CTC is 5 LPA. Eight of the nine students got 7 LPA or less, so the median describes this batch far better than the other two numbers.</p>

  <p>To see what a college's median means for your own fee, total the fee and living cost first with the free <a href="/tools/course-cost-planner/">course cost planner</a>.</p>

  <p>With an even number of students, there is no single middle student. Take the two middle salaries and find their halfway point.</p>

  <p>One catch: the median counts only students who got offers. If a third of a batch got none, the middle student of the whole batch would earn less than the published median.</p>

  <h2 id="this-week">Do this week</h2>
  <p>Use these steps on any college that quotes a highest package. They take one phone call and one calculator.</p>
  <div class="week-box">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Day 1.</strong> Ask the placement cell in writing for three years of branch-wise median CTC and fixed pay, the number of students who sat for placement and the number placed.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 2.</strong> Divide placed by sat. In an example batch of 100 where 60 were placed, the median describes only those 60, and 40 students are missing from it.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 3.</strong> Turn the median CTC into monthly in-hand pay with the <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/tools/ctc-to-in-hand-salary-calculator/">CTC to in-hand salary calculator</a>. Use fixed pay only, not the variable part.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 4.</strong> Divide your total fee by that monthly in-hand pay. The answer is the number of months of pay the fee takes, before any loan interest.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="week-box__note">If a college will not share branch-wise median in writing, treat the headline package as advertising.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="highest-package">Highest package, average and median: what each one answers</h2>

  <p>These three numbers can come from the same batch and tell three different stories. Each one answers a different question.</p>

  <div class="number-grid">
    {numberCards.map((card) => (
      <div class={`number-card number-card--${card.tone}`}>
        <p class="number-card__label">{card.label}</p>
        <p class="number-card__row number-card__row--fit"><strong>Answers:</strong> {card.answers}</p>
        <p class="number-card__row number-card__row--warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> {card.watch}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>A college that shows only the highest package is showing you its best day. A college that shows the median, the average and the lowest offers is showing you the batch.</p>

  <h2 id="real-numbers">A real batch: IIT Bombay, 2024-25</h2>

  <p>IIT Bombay's 2024-25 season shows how far an average can sit above its median, and how a placement percentage changes with the group you divide by. <a href="https://www.freepressjournal.in/education/iit-bombay-average-salary-hits-rs-2645-lakh-1422-students-placed" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Free Press Journal reported</a> these figures from the institute's placement report (page updated 19 May 2026, checked on 17 November 2026).</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Measure</th>
          <th>2024-25 figure</th>
          <th>What it tells you</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {iitRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Measure"><strong>{row.measure}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="2024-25 figure">{row.figure}</td>
            <td data-label="What it tells you">{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The average was 6.45 LPA above the median, so a few very large offers lifted it, and the middle student got less than the average. Divide the same 1,422 offers by the 2,040 who took part and you get about 70%. Divide by the 2,469 who registered and you get about 58%.</p>

  <p>IIT Bombay is one of India's top technical institutes, so these numbers show how the terms work, not what an average college's batch gets.</p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Why the highest package is missing</p>
    <p>For 2024-25, <a href="https://news.careers360.com/iit-placements-2024-2025-no-highest-package-data-bombay-delhi-patna-roorkee-crore-salary-btech-jobs-median-average-lowest-aipc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the All IITs Placement Committee decided not to disclose the highest package</a>, with the stated aim of easing the stress that big package headlines put on students.</p>
    <p>Rankings use the median too. <a href="https://www.nirfindia.org/nirfpdfcdn/2025/framework/Colleges.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIRF's 2025 framework for colleges</a> scores the median salary of graduates, not the highest.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="placement-meaning">What does placement mean in college, in simple words?</h2>

  <p>Placement means a company hires you through your college. Students often hear "highest package" and "placement percentage" together, so it helps to see how the two numbers are made.</p>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    {placementSteps.map((step) => (
      <li><strong>{step.title}.</strong> {step.body}</li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p><strong>An illustration, not a real college.</strong> A batch has 120 students. Eighty register for placements and 72 get offers.</p>
    <p>The college can say 90% placed (72 of 80 registered) or 60% placed (72 of 120 students). Both are true, and only one of them describes your odds.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ctc-not-salary">Why CTC is not your monthly pay</h2>

  <p>CTC means cost to company: what the employer spends on you over a year. Your bank account sees only part of it.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Part of the offer</th>
          <th>Do you get it as cash?</th>
          <th>Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {ctcRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Part of the offer"><strong>{row.part}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Do you get it as cash?">{row.paid}</td>
            <td data-label="Why it matters">{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Tax and your own PF share are cut on top of this. <a href="https://www.weekday.works/post/7-5-lpa-in-hand-salary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">One online calculator for FY 2026-27</a> puts a Rs 7.5 lakh CTC at roughly Rs 53,000 to Rs 57,000 a month in hand, while Rs 7.5 lakh divided by 12 is Rs 62,500. The exact figure depends on how the employer splits the pay.</p>

  <p>To see your own figure, put the CTC from your offer letter into the <a href="/tools/ctc-to-in-hand-salary-calculator/">CTC to in-hand salary calculator</a>.</p>

  <p>Two offers with the same CTC can therefore differ by thousands of rupees a month. Ask each employer for the fixed pay, the variable pay and the in-hand amount in writing.</p>

  <p>The four labour codes <a href="https://www.outlookindia.com/national/indias-new-labour-codes-take-effect-all-you-need-to-know" target="_blank" rel="noopener">took effect on 21 November 2025</a>. Basic pay must now be at least half of total pay, which can raise PF and gratuity cuts and lower take-home pay for some people. Ask for the written breakup before you compare two offers.</p>

  <h2 id="which-number">Which number to use for which decision</h2>

  <p>
    Match the number to the decision. Then remember that every one of these numbers describes a past batch, and the part you can still change is the skill and proof you bring to your own interview.
  </p>

  <div class="decision-stack">
    {decisionRows.map((row, i) => (
      <div class="decision-row">
        <span class="decision-row__num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
        <div>
          <p class="decision-row__title">{row.title}</p>
          <p class="decision-row__body">{row.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h3>Money check before you pay a fee</h3>

  <p>Our strongest suggestion: spend about a tenth of the family's whole education money on the college fee, and stop to check hard if the fee heads past a fifth. Above that line, the proof has to come from your own branch's numbers, not from the brochure. Free and very cheap online courses sit outside this line.</p>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p><strong>An illustration, not a real fee.</strong> A family sets aside Rs 15 lakh for everything: fee, living costs, a laptop, tools and later courses. A tenth is Rs 1.5 lakh, and a fifth is Rs 3 lakh.</p>
    <p>A college then charges Rs 9 lakh in all, with a branch median of Rs 4.2 lakh CTC and Rs 3.6 lakh of fixed pay inside it. That fee is three times the Rs 3 lakh line, and before tax and PF it equals two and a half years of fixed pay.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ask-placement-cell">What to ask the placement cell for</h2>

  <p>You do not need a long audit to get a straight answer. Send these three requests in one message, and see how comfortable the college is with its own numbers.</p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    {askItems.map((item, i) => (
      <div class={`compare-card compare-card--${['teal', 'gold', 'violet'][i]}`}>
        <p class="compare-card__label">Ask {i + 1}</p>
        <p class="compare-card__title">{item.title}</p>
        <p class="compare-card__body">{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="above-median">How to land above the median</h2>

  <p>The company that hires you is the one that pays, and it checks what it can test in a campus round: an aptitude or coding test, a technical interview, and proof that you have done real work. The median tells you what that process paid your seniors, not what it will pay you.</p>

  <p>The first skill worth building is explaining one piece of your own work in two minutes: the problem, what you did, and what changed. A working project suits computer science, a short case note on a real company's numbers suits commerce and MBA, and a design or lab report suits core engineering.</p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>A high median does not guarantee a high offer, and a low one does not cap you. Pick one core skill for the role you want, add one skill that multiplies it (clear writing or speaking is a common one), and ask two people who work in that field to check your proof. None of this guarantees an offer, but it is the part of the outcome you control.</p>
  </div>

  <p>To check a whole report, not only these two numbers, read <a href="/blog/college-degrees/how-to-read-college-placement-report-india/">how to read a college placement report</a>. To line up several colleges, use <a href="/blog/college-degrees/how-to-compare-colleges-for-career-outcome-india/">how to compare colleges for career outcome</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQs on highest package and median CTC</h2>

  <div class="faq-accordion">
    {faqItems.map((item) => (
      <details class="faq-item" open>
        <summary>{item.q}</summary>
        <div class="faq-a">{item.a}</div>
      </details>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="next-step">Next step</h2>

  <p>Ask your shortlisted colleges for the branch-wise median CTC and fixed pay, then set them against the full cost of the course. While you wait for the replies, start one piece of work you can show, because no placement report can give you that.</p>

  <p><strong>Try it now:</strong> <a href="/tools/education-cost-payback-planner/">Use the free education cost payback planner</a> to set a college's fees against the median pay you can expect.</p>


  <p>If the campus drive looks thin, <a href="/blog/job-search/campus-vs-off-campus-placement-india/">campus vs off-campus placement</a> shows when going outside it is the smarter route.</p>

  <h2 id="sources">Sources checked</h2>

  <p>Figures were read between 17 and 22 November 2026. Each source shows its own date, and placement figures describe a past batch.</p>

